          "letter": "",
          "english": "In a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.",
          "chinese": "研究人员通过一系列实验展示了我们如今在多大程度上开始欣赏忙碌，或者至少欣赏忙碌的表象。志愿者阅读两段文字，一段讲述一名过着悠闲生活的男子，另一段讲述一名工作过度、日程排得满满当当的男子；当被要求判断两人的社会地位谁更高时，大多数参与者都选择了后者。对于使用那些暗示自己时间紧缺的产品的人，结果也一样：例如，在一项实验中，Peapod 杂货配送服务的顾客被认为比在价格同样昂贵的杂货店购物的人社会地位更高；在另一项实验中，即使无线耳机和普通耳机都只是用来听音乐，佩戴无线耳机的人仍被认为比佩戴普通耳机的人处于更高的社会阶层。",
